    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful. To wrap this up, what happens in cases where the disruption isn't a single massive event, but rather a series of small, unpredictable, and chaotic disruptions that cumulatively make it impossible to run the business? In legal terms, how would a court handle that kind of cumulative, unpredictable \"chaos\" when evaluating if it meets the force majeure or frustration criteria?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In cases where a business faces a series of small, unpredictable, and chaotic disruptions that cumulatively make it impossible to operate, the legal concept of frustration may come into play. Frustration occurs when an unforeseen event fundamentally changes the nature of the contractual obligations, rendering them impossible to perform or radically different from what was agreed upon.\n\nIn Singapore, for a court to find that a contract has been frustrated, the event must be beyond the control of the parties, not due to the fault of either party, and must render the performance of the contract impossible or illegal. The court will consider whether the cumulative effect of the disruptions was foreseeable at the time the contract was entered into and whether the parties could have reasonably anticipated and provided for such a scenario in the contract.\n\nIf the disruptions are deemed to be cumulative and unpredictable, and they have indeed made it impossible to run the business, the court may find that the contract has been frustrated. This would release both parties from their contractual obligations. However, the court will carefully examine the nature of the disruptions, the terms of the contract, and any relevant provisions that may address such situations.\n\nThe tenant would need to provide evidence that the disruptions were not only unpredictable but also that they had a significant and adverse impact on",
